                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e71ae68b-1871-370a-b064-6de72a67312e",
                        "entry_id": "d6cdbf1c-9363-34f7-ac6e-b036eb0cec62",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Sentence populaire, par laquelle les Syracusains condamnaient à l’exil, pour cinq ans, ceux d’entre leurs concitoyens dont la puissance paraissait dangereuse pour la liberté publique."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Ancient"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9876eee1-062a-48ad-bbbf-7823102fb335",
                                "sense_id": "e71ae68b-1871-370a-b064-6de72a67312e",
                                "text": "Nous savons aussi qu’existait à Syracuse une procédure analogue à celle de l’ostracisme athénien, le pétalisme, qui permettait de chasser de la cité quiconque menaçait de rétablir à son profit la tyrannie."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a1ced7f5-8cab-4d18-9f54-5683945d65cb",
                                "sense_id": "e71ae68b-1871-370a-b064-6de72a67312e",
                                "text": "On a beaucoup évoqué l’ostracisme, en bien et en mal, depuis qu’en 1791, l’Académie des inscriptions et belles-lettres a mis au concours l’étude des avantages et des inconvénients de l’ostracisme et de l’institution analogue que connaissait Syracuse, le « pétalisme »."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "dd5b7f47-1de6-4982-8b23-d28fb837e750",
                        "source_entry_id": "d6cdbf1c-9363-34f7-ac6e-b036eb0cec62",
                        "source_sense_id": "e71ae68b-1871-370a-b064-6de72a67312e",
                        "target_lang_word": "petalism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "petalism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3bc58e6c-3513-38e9-9ce8-907013ffc1e4",
                                        "entry_id": "33c21dd1-8dc6-369d-ac5e-7b552cd00045",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A form of ostracism among the ancient Syracusans by which they temporarily banished a citizen suspected of having dangerous influence or ambition."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "historical",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cb4d52af-f9bc-44fd-8be4-6dcac9ae0099",
                                                "sense_id": "3bc58e6c-3513-38e9-9ce8-907013ffc1e4",
                                                "text": "Witnesse the Ostracisme amongst the Athenians, and the Petalisme among the Siracusans."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6f63f1f6-8bb7-4744-85ff-143b29b28b6c",
                                                "sense_id": "3bc58e6c-3513-38e9-9ce8-907013ffc1e4",
                                                "text": "They've identified a reference to the ancient Syracusian practice of banishment known as petalism, and what may or may not be a reference to efilism, a branch of the antinatalist philosophical position advanced by Schopenhauer, but what any of that has to do with the lyrics' subsequent allusions to Moby-Dick seems pretty open to question."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
